# AGENTS.md

Instructions for AI coding agents (Claude Code, Cursor, Codex and others) working
in this project. It was generated by [Layrgo](https://github.com/samznd/layrgo);
keep it up to date as the project grows.

## Project

- Module: `{{.Module}}`
- HTTP: {{.FrameworkName}}, listening on `:3000`, API under `/api/v1`
- Database: {{.DatabaseName}}, accessed through {{.ORMName}}
- Configuration comes from environment variables, loaded from `.env` in
  development. `.env.example` lists them all.

## Commands

```bash
go run ./cmd                # start the server (needs the database in .env)
go build ./...
go vet ./...
go test ./...
gofmt -l .                  # must print nothing
docker compose up --build   # app and database together
```

A change is done only when `gofmt -l .` prints nothing and `go vet ./...`,
`go build ./...` and `go test ./...` all pass.

## Layout

```
cmd/main.go           entry point: load config, connect, wire features, start the server
config/database.go    opens the database pool (config.DB) and nothing else
internal/<feature>/   one package per feature:
    model.go            the feature's types and errors
    handler.go          HTTP only: parse input, call the service, write the response
    service.go          business rules and validation
    service_test.go     unit tests for the service, with a fake store
    store.go            data access, the only file with SQL or ORM calls
    routes.go           SetupRoutes, the feature's URL table
internal/middleware/  shared HTTP middleware
pkg/utils/            small helpers with no project dependencies
```

`internal/message` is the sample feature. Copy its shape for new features. Its
store creates and seeds its own table in `Migrate`, which `cmd/main.go` calls at
startup; every feature that owns a table needs a `Migrate` like it. When
you no longer need it, delete the package and its wiring and route in
`cmd/main.go`.

## Rules

1. **Dependencies point one way: handler → service → store.** A handler never
   touches `config.DB` or SQL. A store never sees HTTP types. A service knows
   neither.
2. **Each layer depends on a small unexported interface that it declares
   itself**, listing only the methods it calls (see `messageService` in
   `internal/message/handler.go` and `store` in `internal/message/service.go`).
   Constructors take that interface, so tests can pass fakes.
3. **Pass the request's `context.Context` all the way down to the database
   call.**
4. **Only `cmd/main.go` reads `config.DB`.** Everything else gets its
   dependencies through a constructor.
5. **Wrap errors with context** (`fmt.Errorf("get bookmark %d: %w", id, err)`).
   The store turns "no row" into the feature's `ErrNotFound`, and handlers map
   errors to status codes with `errors.Is`. Never send internal error text to
   clients.
6. **Don't add or upgrade dependencies** unless the task needs it. `go.mod` pins
   versions that are known to work together, so do not run `go get -u`.
7. Never commit `.env`. When you add a variable, add it to `.env.example` too.
8. **Every feature gets tests.** At minimum, test the service's rules with a
   fake store, as `internal/message/service_test.go` does.
{{- if eq .Framework "fiber"}}
9. This project uses Fiber **v3** (`github.com/gofiber/fiber/v3`), pinned to a
   beta. Handlers take `fiber.Ctx`, an interface, not `*fiber.Ctx`. Most Fiber
   examples online are for v2 and will not compile here.
{{- end}}

## Adding a feature

Run `layrgo add feature <name> --fields "…" --yes`, then fill in the `TODO`s.
Don't copy `internal/message` by hand when the command is available.

```bash
layrgo add feature order --fields "customer:string,note:text?,total:int,paid:bool" --yes
```

It writes `internal/order/` (model, store, service, service test, handler and
routes) for this project's stack, wires it into `cmd/main.go` above the
`// layrgo:wire` and `// layrgo:routes` markers, and runs gofmt, build, vet and
the feature's tests. Field types are `string`, `text`, `int`, `float`, `bool` and
`time`; a trailing `?` makes a field optional. Every feature gets an `id`. Add
`--dry-run` to preview, `--json` for machine-readable output. Exit codes: 0 done,
1 invalid input, 2 the feature exists, 3 `cmd/main.go` needs the printed lines
pasted, 4 a check failed. Keep the markers in `cmd/main.go`.

The walkthrough below is the reference for what the command generates, and for
changes it does not cover. It adds a `bookmark` feature with `POST /api/v1/bookmarks` and
`GET /api/v1/bookmarks/{id}`. Rename it for your feature.

